{"name":"Cerulean Tint","hex":"#acbfcd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cerulean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A greenish-blue color.","Any of various lycaenid butterflies of the genus Jamides."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sky-blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cerulean"},{"term":"tint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slight coloring.","A pale or faint tinge of any color; especially, a variation of a color obtained by adding white (contrast shade)"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shade, to color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tint"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cerulean","blocks":[{"text":"The color cerulean, or caerulean, is a variety of the hue of blue that may range from a light azure blue to a more intense sky blue. Cerulean may also be mixed with the hue of green. The first recorded use of cerulean as a color name in English was in 1590. The word is derived from the Latin word caeruleus, \"dark blue, blue, or blue-green\", which in turn probably derives from caerulum, diminutive of caelum, \"heaven, sky\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerulean"},{"title":"Tint, shade and tone","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, a tint is a mixture of a color with white, which increases lightness, while a shade is a mixture with black, which increases darkness. A tone is produced either by mixing a color with gray, or by both tinting and shading. Mixing a color with any neutral color reduces the chroma, or colorfulness, while the perceived hue can be affected slightly."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Tint-tone-shade.svg/330px-Tint-tone-shade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tint%2C_shade_and_tone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/PB35_Bleu_C%C3%A9rul%C3%A9um.JPG/250px-PB35_Bleu_C%C3%A9rul%C3%A9um.J